CORE gnu-extensions
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ store then load: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ exchange returns the old value: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ fetch_add: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ bool: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.5\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
is unknown$
dropped the body
--
Clang's __c11_atomic_* intrinsics are what libc++'s <atomic> is built on
(and <stdatomic.h> in C).  `T __c11_atomic_load(_Atomic(T) *, int)' and the
store/exchange/fetch_OP intrinsics are polymorphic in the atomic object's
type, exactly like GCC's __atomic_load_n & co. (GCC manual, "Built-in
Functions for Memory Model Aware Atomic Operations"); they are typed and
implemented as those.  Previously they were declared as varargs functions
returning void, so every libc++ atomic operation on a non-void type failed
("invalid implicit conversion from 'void' to 'bool'") and the enclosing
__cxx_atomic_load/exchange bodies were dropped (144 drops over the libc++
tests of the corpus).
